def cmd_graph_conformance(args: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
    """T-12144 (SPEC-0190 rule 10) — the ONE request-scoped ReadScope for the `graph conformance` seam.

    THE SHAPE IT REMOVES, MEASURED ON THIS REPO 2026-09-05: two of this seam's composed report-only
    views — `_test_building_warnings` and `_uncatalogued_type_warnings` — each fold the WHOLE logical
    journal independently, so one `graph conformance` read all 96 segments TWICE (192 physical folds,
    folds_per_segment 2.000). That is the steady state, not an outlier: every production row for this
    verb in the 7-day window sits at exactly 2.000 on that axis, and the rule-37 line's 8.042x peak is
    the same two folds plus a dispatch-union read on top.

    IT REUSES THE EXISTING PRIMITIVE (CHARTER §P1 filter 1) — `journal_mod.rows_memo([EVENTS_PATH])` is
    the T-11453 scope already installed at `_debt_echo_lines` and `_advisory_read_scope`; this is a
    third wiring site for it, never a per-view cache (rule 10's named retirement). The declaration
    names the LOGICAL journal, so the memo resolves its own segment set and no site maintains a
    physical path list that goes stale the next time rotation adds a segment. Nothing survives the
    scope, and every reader outside it streams exactly as before.

    The card side needs no scope: `state.load_path` is already memoised, so this seam's 4,542 card
    loads resolve to 3,436 distinct parses — card_parse_ratio 1.0, at the floor already.

    T-12206 — THE READ THE SCOPE WAS MISSING, AND WHY IT SITS HERE. Collapsing the composed views left
    this seam at 1.000 folds-per-segment for a session whose carried ref is BACKED, and at 7.041 for one
    whose ref is not: the residue is not a view at all but the SESSION-IDENTITY read
    `_emit_cli_invoked` takes AFTER the verb body, in `main`'s `finally`. On an UNBACKED carry
    `_ref_current_epoch_anchored` pays the whole `_READ_GATE_RETRY_ATTEMPTS` budget of FULL journal
    walks by design (a refusal is always decided on complete evidence), and every one of them landed
    OUTSIDE this scope. Measured on the engine 2026-09-07 with the nightly's own env: 690 physical folds
    of 98 segments and 3,927,626 parses of a 644k-row journal, 43.2 s — against 98 folds / 0 parses /
    1.5 s for the same command from a backed session. That is the whole gap between this seam's 8.042x
    peak and its 1.000x median, and it is why the peak is the NIGHTLY's row (`cron-yitc-v2-nightly`,
    2026-09-04/05/06) rather than any interactive one.

    So the identity read JOINS the scope rather than getting a scope of its own — rule 10's wiring-site
    placement applied to the read that was left out, never a second memo and never a per-view cache. It
    is PRE-WARMED here, inside the scope, because the emit site is generic across every `cli_invoked`
    verb in `main` and is not this seam's to wrap; the later emit-time call then costs nothing.

    IT CANNOT CHANGE WHAT THE ROW SAYS, and that is a property of the existing machinery, not of care
    taken here. The answer is DISCARDED — `_emit_cli_invoked` still resolves the ref itself — so this
    only ever populates `_REF_BACKING_UNBACKED`, whose key is `(ref, journal size, mtime_ns)`. A journal
    that moved between the two calls MISSES that memo and is re-decided live, exactly as today; a torn
    fold moves the same signature, so a truncated read can never be stored and then honoured. The
    T-10115 retry budget and the SPEC-0137 Rule 2.1 fail-closed direction are both untouched."""
    # T-13307: the two journal-reading report views share ONE request-scoped answer
    # (`_conformance_journal_views`), saved/restored like the memo so nothing survives the request.
    prior_holder = getattr(_CONF_JOURNAL_TLS, "holder", None)
    _CONF_JOURNAL_TLS.holder = {}
    try:
        with journal_mod.rows_memo([EVENTS_PATH]):
            # Best-effort by construction: `_try_resolve_session_ref` is the NON-DYING twin (T-10167) and
            # this call is a pre-warm whose result is thrown away, so ANY failure here must degrade to the
            # read simply happening later, at the emit site, as it does today.
            try:
                _try_resolve_session_ref()
            except Exception:   # noqa: BLE001 — a pre-warm must never break the verb it rides on
                pass
            return _cmd_graph_conformance_scoped(args)
    finally:
        _CONF_JOURNAL_TLS.holder = prior_holder


def _cmd_graph_conformance_scoped(args: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
    # T-10297 (SPEC-0150): the executable-key ownership + read-to-effect chain check joins the conformance
    # RED set. It is composed HERE, at the host seam, rather than threaded through the graph core's already
    # 30-argument injection list: the check reads the born template + live kernel SOURCE (host state), which
    # the pure graph core never touches. Ordering: run the ownership check FIRST and print its violations, so
    # they are visible even when the graph core's own RED exits before returning; then re-raise the harsher
    # of the two exit codes (a RED anywhere is a RED).
    # T-10298 (SPEC-0151): the anchor-rule sweep — one REPORT-ONLY line per active spec that mandates a
    # behavior while naming nothing that executes it. Printed FIRST (before any RED can exit) and
    # deliberately OUTSIDE the RED set: SPEC-0151 §Internal 3 starts report-only, and hardening to a
    # blocking gate needs its own incident (CHARTER §P1 F4). It never touches the exit code below.
    ar_warn = _anchor_rule_warnings()
    for w in ar_warn:
        print(f"  anchor-rule: {w['spec']} {w['detail']}", file=sys.stderr)
    if ar_warn:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: WARN — {len(ar_warn)} active spec(s) mandate a behavior with no execution "
              f"anchor (SPEC-0151, report-only); see above.", file=sys.stderr)
    # T-10349 (SPEC-0127 / Bug B): the diff-aware seed-budget WARN — report-only, OUTSIDE the RED set and
    # never touching the exit code (the consult forbade a second authoring gate). Pairs with the always-
    # loaded AGENTS §Authoring the seed cue: a growing seed SOURCE doc prompts "does this rule body belong
    # in its SPEC, with the seed carrying cue+pointer only? is every new heading audience-tagged?".
    sg = _seed_growth_warnings()
    for w in sg["growth"]:
        # T-10661: SIGNED deltas ({:+d}) — a BYTE-triggered finding (bytes grew, lines held or FELL:
        # the density growth the line-only trigger missed) legitimately carries a 0/negative line
        # delta, so a hardcoded '+' would render "+-3 line(s)". Never template a literal '+'.
        print(f"  seed-growth: {w['doc']} {w['delta']:+d} line(s) / {w['byte_delta']:+d} byte(s) "
              f"~{w['tok_delta']:+d} tok vs merge-base "
              f"({w['base_lines']}→{w['cur_lines']} lines, {w['base_bytes']}→{w['cur_bytes']} bytes)",
              file=sys.stderr)
    for w in sg["untagged"]:
        print(f"  seed-untagged: {w['doc']} heading {w['heading']!r} has no own AUDIENCE marker "
              f"(defaults to core = loaded by everyone; tag it core|controller|worker, SPEC-0127 §1a)",
              file=sys.stderr)
    # T-10661: the AGGREGATE trajectory — a REPORTED OUTCOME beside the 2500-LINE cap, printed only
    # when this diff actually grew the set in bytes. Deliberately NO byte/token threshold to compare
    # against and NO decreed token number (owner ruling Q1, 2026-07-17): a decreed number is
    # voluntaristic — the three-bucket PLACEMENT TEST decides what stays, and this line only makes the
    # trajectory visible. Report-only like everything on this seam (stderr, exit code untouched).
    tot = sg.get("totals")
    if tot and tot["byte_delta"] > 0:
        print(f"  seed-budget: set now {tot['cur_lines']} line(s) of the 2500 cap "
              f"({tot['cur_lines'] * 100 // 2500}%) / {tot['cur_bytes'] // 1024}KB ~{tot['tok_est'] // 1000}k tok "
              f"— this diff {tot['line_delta']:+d} line(s) / {tot['byte_delta']:+d} byte(s) "
              f"~{tot['tok_delta']:+d} tok (reported outcome + trajectory, NOT a cap — CHARTER §P2)",
              file=sys.stderr)
    # T-12746: the RETRIEVED-tier half of the SAME family — the stage bundles `stage <NAME>` delivers.
    # The seed half above watches CANONICAL_DOCS only, which is why SPEC-0036 could go 47047 B → 115546 B
    # and SPEC-0204 be born at 95205 B with no signal anywhere (T-12473). Report-only on identical terms:
    # stderr, OUTSIDE the RED set, exit code untouched, NO threshold and NO decreed number (owner ruling
    # Q1, 2026-07-17) — a trajectory made visible, never a cap. The LINES are built by the pure
    # `graph.bundle_growth_lines` so the render itself is directly probeable (audit-post RED, 2026-09-19):
    # this seam only chooses the stream, never the text.
    for _line in graph.bundle_growth_lines(sg.get("bundle_growth") or [],
                                           sg.get("bundle_totals"), STAGE_AXIS_NAMES):
        print(_line, file=sys.stderr)
    # T-12862: the over-ceiling member of the same family — ANY durable doc already past the SPEC-0120
    # must-split ceiling that this diff grew in place, whatever tier delivers it. Same report-only terms.
    for _line in inspection.ceiling_growth_lines(sg.get("ceiling_growth") or []):
        print(_line, file=sys.stderr)
    if sg["growth"] or sg["untagged"] or (sg.get("bundle_growth") or []) or (sg.get("ceiling_growth") or []):
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: WARN — {len(sg['growth'])} seed SOURCE doc(s) grew vs the merge-base + "
              f"{len(sg['untagged'])} untagged seed heading(s) (authoring-time audience/seed-budget check, "
              f"SPEC-0127, report-only): confirm new normative rule bodies live in their SPEC (seed = "
              f"cue+pointer only) and every #/## heading is audience-tagged; see above. "
              f"+ {len(sg.get('bundle_growth') or [])} stage-delivered spec(s) grew (T-12746, same "
              f"report-only terms — the retrieved tier the seed half cannot see) + "
              f"{len(sg.get('ceiling_growth') or [])} over-ceiling durable doc(s) grew in place "
              f"(T-12862, SPEC-0120 §3 — split, do not grow).", file=sys.stderr)
    # T-10680 (SPEC-0165 rule 5 — extend-before-create): the report-only test-building sweep. OUTSIDE
    # the RED set and never touching the exit code — SPEC-0165 rule 5 is a folding CUE ("before writing
    # a new test file, extend the rule's existing suite"), a loud tripwire OR an explicit waive, never a
    # second gate (CHARTER §P1; a genuinely new rule justifies a new file). The `test-growth` line is the
    # paired growth-bend probe data — a reported outcome + trajectory, printed unconditionally so it is
    # derivable by this one command; NOT a cap (sibling of the seed-budget line above).
    tb = _test_building_warnings()
    for w in tb["new_files"]:
        print(f"  test-new-file: tests/{w['file']} is a NEW standalone test file — before creating one, "
              f"EXTEND the rule's existing suite (SPEC-0165 rule 5 extend-before-create); a genuinely NEW "
              f"rule justifies a new file, else fold the probe into the rule's suite. Report-only.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    # T-12023 (X-1248): the line itself is rendered by the PURE `graph.test_growth_line`, which states a
    # SKIP for the consumer sentinel (no numbers, no anchoring instruction) and leaves the engine line
    # byte-unchanged — an absent reading must never render as a measured zero.
    print(graph.test_growth_line(tb["counts"]), file=sys.stderr)
    if tb["new_files"]:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: WARN — {len(tb['new_files'])} new standalone test file(s) added vs the "
              f"merge-base (SPEC-0165 rule 5 extend-before-create, report-only): confirm each is a "
              f"genuinely new rule, else fold its probe into the rule's existing suite; see above.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    # T-12398 (X-1368): the named-consumer-example sweep — report-only, OUTSIDE the RED set, never
    # touching the exit code (the `test-new-file` / `uncatalogued-type` shape). A kernel rule that names
    # a registered consumer as its illustrative case takes on that consumer's state as an unrecorded
    # maintenance obligation: SPEC-0109 §5 and SPEC-0110 rule 1 both named <project> as the WAIVE case,
    # both went false when <project> started running a collector, and <project>'s own waiver reason then
    # cited the stale kernel example back as its justification. Never a gate — naming a real project is
    # sometimes exactly right (a positive declares-case) — so this PROMPTS the author to ask whether the
    # example rots, and the count line hands the Controller the wider-sweep decision.
    nce = _named_consumer_example_warnings()
    for w in nce:
        print(f"  named-consumer-example: {w['spec']}:{w['line']} names the registered consumer "
              f"{w['name']!r} as an illustrative case in a numbered rule body — {w['text']!r}. A rule "
              f"that names a project inherits that project's state as a maintenance obligation nobody "
              f"records (X-1368: the example went false and then SHIELDED a false consumer "
              f"declaration). Prefer project-neutral phrasing. Report-only.", file=sys.stderr)
    # Printed UNCONDITIONALLY (the `test-growth` shape): a silent probe and a dead probe are otherwise
    # indistinguishable, and the remaining-corpus count is what AC3 hands the Controller.
    print(f"  named-consumer-example: {len(nce)} illustrative consumer-name mention(s) in ACTIVE kernel "
          f"spec rule bodies (X-1368 rot probe — reported outcome, NOT a gate).", file=sys.stderr)
    if nce:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: WARN — {len(nce)} ACTIVE kernel spec rule body(ies) name a registered "
              f"consumer as an illustrative case (X-1368, report-only): confirm each is a rule the "
              f"project's state cannot falsify, else re-phrase it project-neutrally; see above.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    # T-11379: the event-catalog RESIDUE — report-only, OUTSIDE the RED set, never touching the exit
    # code. The gate (tests/test_event_catalog_completeness.py) is judged on the DIFF since T-11379, so a
    # type uncatalogued at the merge-base is unattributable to any branch and is NOT failed; unreported it
    # would simply vanish, which is the silent-narrowing this line exists to prevent. Naming it here keeps
    # the honest bound visible without any land being refused on it.
    ut = _uncatalogued_type_warnings()
    for ty in ut["inherited"]:
        print(f"  uncatalogued-type: {ty} is a live event type no active spec homes, ALREADY uncatalogued "
              f"at the merge-base — inherited, so no branch's diff is failed for it (T-11379). Home it in "
              f"a spec (SPEC-0161 is the append-only home) or name it a SPEC-0161 no-emitter deferral. "
              f"Report-only.", file=sys.stderr)
    for ty in ut["introduced"]:
        print(f"  uncatalogued-type: {ty} is INTRODUCED by this diff and uncatalogued — the land-verify "
              f"event-catalog gate WILL fail this branch on it (T-11379). Home it in a spec now.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    if ut["inherited"] or ut["introduced"]:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: WARN — {len(ut['inherited'])} inherited + {len(ut['introduced'])} "
              f"diff-introduced uncatalogued live event type(s) (SPEC-0161 federated catalog, T-11379, "
              f"report-only); see above.", file=sys.stderr)
    ko_viol = _key_ownership_violations()
    for v in ko_viol:
        print(f"  {v['kind']}: {v['detail']}", file=sys.stderr)
    if ko_viol:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(ko_viol)} carrier-key ownership violation(s) "
              f"(SPEC-0150); see above.", file=sys.stderr)
    # T-10984 (SPEC-0128 Rule 6): the mandatory-flip MIGRATION guard joins the conformance RED set. A
    # concern may not carry `presence_policy: mandatory` unless the same corpus supplies an UNANSWERED
    # scaffold AND probe evidence that a PRE-CHANGE carrier receives it. Composed at THIS host seam for
    # the `_key_ownership_violations` reason above: it reads the born template and RUNS the shipped
    # `init --backfill-mandatory` delivery (host state the pure graph core never touches). It is a RED,
    # not a WARN, because the incident it prevents is silent by construction — spike_sandbox and
    # spike_data_safety flipped to mandatory and nine existing consumers were stranded with no way to be
    # asked, discovered only by a later audit (T-10982).
    mm = init_mod.mandatory_migration_violations()
    mm_viol = mm["violations"]
    for v in mm_viol:
        print(f"  {v['kind']}: {v['detail']}", file=sys.stderr)
    if mm_viol:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(mm_viol)} mandatory concern(s) without a proven migration "
              f"(SPEC-0128 Rule 6); see above.", file=sys.stderr)
    elif mm["proven"]:
        # Never a bare silence: name what the guard actually exercised, so a green line is evidence the
        # probe RAN rather than evidence it found nothing to run (the vacuous-control failure mode).
        print(f"  mandatory-migration: {len(mm['proven'])} mandatory concern(s) proven migratable to a "
              f"PRE-CHANGE carrier ({', '.join(mm['proven'])}) — SPEC-0128 Rule 6.", file=sys.stderr)
    # T-11968 (SPEC-0189 rule 1): the BORN-PERMISSIVE DETECTOR PAIRING guard joins the same RED set. A
    # concern may not declare `born_stance: permissive` unless the same corpus NAMES the detector that
    # watches for that absence starting to cost something. Composed at THIS host seam for the reason the
    # two guards above are: it reads the derived registry off disk (host state the pure graph core never
    # touches). It is a RED, not a WARN, because rule 1 makes the PAIR the unit of admission — a warning
    # would leave precisely the unenforced convention that rule forbids, and the failure it permits is
    # silent by construction: a born-permissive default surfaces nothing on its own, so the missing
    # detector disables the very review that would later catch it.
    bp = init_mod.born_permissive_detector_violations()
    bp_viol = bp["violations"]
    for v in bp_viol:
        print(f"  {v['kind']}: {v['detail']}", file=sys.stderr)
    if bp_viol:
        # KIND-ACCURATE SUMMARY (T-11972): this guard now returns TWO refusal families — rule 1's
        # missing detector and rule 7+8's LIVE born declaration unauthorized by the CHARTER
        # amendment. Each gets its OWN verdict line, counted over its own kind, so neither is
        # misreported as the other. Rule 1's sentence is preserved VERBATIM: `tests/
        # test_detector_naming.py` pins it as the guard's refusal wording, and that pin is the
        # definer's contract — a card registering a second family may add a line, never re-word an
        # existing one.
        _no_det = [v for v in bp_viol if v.get("kind") == "concern-permissive-born-no-detector"]
        _unauth = [v for v in bp_viol
                   if v.get("kind") == "concern-permissive-born-unauthorized-by-charter"]
        if _no_det:
            print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(_no_det)} born-permissive concern(s) naming no detector "
                  f"(SPEC-0189 rule 1); see above.", file=sys.stderr)
        if _unauth:
            print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(_unauth)} born-permissive concern(s) shipping a LIVE born "
                  f"declaration that the CHARTER amendment does not authorize (SPEC-0189 rules 7+8); "
                  f"see above.", file=sys.stderr)
        _other = [v for v in bp_viol if v not in _no_det and v not in _unauth]
        if _other:
            print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(_other)} born-permissive concern violation(s) "
                  f"[{', '.join(sorted({str(v.get('kind') or '?') for v in _other}))}]; see above.",
                  file=sys.stderr)
    elif bp["proven"]:
        # Never a bare silence, for the `mandatory-migration` reason above: name what was exercised, so a
        # green line is evidence the guard RAN rather than evidence it found nothing to run. A corpus with
        # no permissive concern at all prints nothing — honestly "nothing to check", not "all clear".
        print(f"  born-permissive-detector: {len(bp['proven'])} permissive born default(s) each naming a "
              f"detector ({', '.join(bp['proven'])}) — SPEC-0189 rule 1.", file=sys.stderr)
    # T-12291 (SPEC-0204 rule 6 / VP6): the RETIRED-AUDIT-FLAGS absence probe joins the same RED set. The
    # retired post-ceiling `audit` surfaces (`--owner-reset`, `--reopen`, the `--task --stage` consult
    # grammar) must STAY absent from the CLI's own `--help`; C5 (T-12290) removed them and left refusal
    # shims, and until this probe nothing watched the surface — a later card could re-register one and no
    # land-verify check would fire. Composed HERE, at the host seam, for the reason the three guards above
    # are: it reads the live `build_parser()` help text and the `bin/lib/audit.py` retirement carriers —
    # host state the pure graph core never touches (its injection list is NOT extended). A RED, not a WARN,
    # because rule 6 asks for a probe ASSERTING the absence, and a warning is exactly the silently-skippable
    # shape the retirement replaced. Printed on STDOUT (the card's probe surface, alongside the graph core's
    # own RED lines) and BEFORE the core call, so the findings are visible even when the core exits first.
    ra_viol = graph.retired_audit_flag_violations(
        _audit_help_texts(), audit.RETIRED_AUDIT_FLAGS, audit.RETIRED_AUDIT_SURFACES)
    for v in ra_viol:
        # The TOKEN is printed beside the detail, not only inside it: AC1 requires the RED to NAME the
        # offending token, and the grammar rule's detail describes a FORM rather than quoting a flag.
        print(f"  {v['kind']}: {v['token']} — {v['detail']}")
    if ra_viol:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(ra_viol)} retired `audit` surface(s) present in the CLI "
              f"`--help` (SPEC-0204 rule 6); see above.")
    # T-12335 (SPEC-0204 plan-gate arm, AC3) — the SIBLING absence probe, on the SAME RED set and for the
    # same reason: rule 6's plan-gate arm retires the `malformed-exhausted` terminal for a plan gate, and
    # nothing watched whether it stayed retired. The LIVE set is DERIVED in `bin/lib/audit.py` from the
    # retirement carrier the refusal shim also reads, so this probe fires the moment the retirement is
    # removed rather than restating its own answer.
    pt_viol = graph.retired_plan_gate_terminal_violations(
        audit.plan_gate_live_terminal_reasons(), audit.RETIRED_PLAN_GATE_TERMINAL_REASONS)
    for v in pt_viol:
        print(f"  {v['kind']}: {v['reason']} — {v['detail']}")
    if pt_viol:
        print(f"CONFORMANCE: RED — {len(pt_viol)} retired plan-gate terminal reason(s) still reachable "
              f"(SPEC-0204 rule 6, plan-gate arm); see above.")
    try:
        graph.cmd_graph_conformance(args, BINDING_RESIDENCY_TOKENS=BINDING_RESIDENCY_TOKENS, BINDING_VERB_SURFACES=BINDING_VERB_SURFACES, CANONICAL_DOCS=CANONICAL_DOCS, PLAN_STAGE_SEQUENCE=PLAN_STAGE_SEQUENCE, REPO_ROOT=REPO_ROOT, SPECS_DIR=SPECS_DIR, STAGE_AXIS_NAMES=STAGE_AXIS_NAMES, _activation_checklist_drift=_activation_checklist_drift, _audience_views_drift=_audience_views_drift, _build_binding_views=_build_binding_views, _concern_registry_drift=_concern_registry_drift, _born_ops_drift=_born_ops_drift, _derived_routing_backing=_derived_routing_backing, _die=_die, _floor_map_drift=_floor_map_drift, _handbook_binding_mirror_underlist=_handbook_binding_mirror_underlist, _handbook_routing_duplication=_handbook_routing_duplication, _handbook_verb_drift=_handbook_verb_drift, _live_cli_verbs=_live_cli_verbs, _one_source_delivery_check=_one_source_delivery_check, _plan_delivery_gating_stages=_plan_delivery_gating_stages, _read_order_drift=_read_order_drift, _read_yaml=_read_yaml, _release_spec_slice_violations=_release_spec_slice_violations, _release_view_drift=_release_view_drift, _relocated_gating_stages=_relocated_gating_stages, _stage_bundle_coverage_violations=_stage_bundle_coverage_violations, _unmarked_cut_card_flags=_unmarked_cut_card_flags, _cross_dangling_resolved=_cross_dangling_resolved, _phantom_activation_owners=_phantom_activation_owners)
    except SystemExit as e:
        raise SystemExit(e.code or (1 if (ko_viol or mm_viol or bp_viol or ra_viol or pt_viol) else 0))
    if ko_viol or mm_viol or bp_viol or ra_viol or pt_viol:
        sys.exit(1)


def _graph_report_verbose() -> bool:
    """T-9715: opt-in for the FULL per-item stale-spec/anchor drift detail in the auto-rebuild
    report. Default OFF (quiet — a single summary counter-line); set YITC_GRAPH_VERBOSE to a truthy
    value (1/true/yes, case-insensitive) to restore the full block. Report-only output gate ONLY —
    never affects the rebuild/conformance verdict or exit code."""
    return os.environ.get("YITC_GRAPH_VERBOSE", "").strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes")


def _auto_rebuild_graph(trigger: str):   # -> dict | None (T-0274: returns the built index, or None on failure)
    """Rebuild graph/index.json as а CLI-workflow side-effect (per T-0026 + D-0022 staleness incident).

    Failure-tolerant: catches broad Exception, emits warning к stderr, never raises.
    Rationale: invoking command (task close / audit) already succeeded + emitted its
    canonical event; stale graph is less harmful than blocking the primary action.

    NOT а git hook / pre-commit gate — runs inline в CLI command flow per
    CHARTER §What V2 is NOT («NOT а hook-laden enforcement platform»).
    """
    try:
        t0 = time.monotonic()   # T-0375: build wall (index build + write)
        index = graph_build_index()
        _write_graph_index(index)   # T-0365: JSON index + floor map via the single write helper
        # T-9513 (E-0034): ALSO regenerate the identity-agnostic release-view export here — release-view
        # is a PURELY-DERIVED artifact (like graph/index.json above), NOT an authored canonical file like
        # AGENTS.md / the placement manifest (those stay explicit-build-only so land never clobbers author
        # edits). Without this a canonical-handbook change landed without a prior `graph build` left main's
        # release-view stale — and a -C consumer READS the release-view as its methodology seed (SPEC-0074
        # §7), so the staleness was consumer-visible. The regenerated paths are in _BOOKKEEPING_ALLOWLIST +
        # _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS, so land folds + auto-resolves them (the "alongside graph/index.json"
        # derived-regen path). Engine-only + complete-handbook guard (the SAME guard cmd_graph_build uses);
        # idempotent (write_text_atomic → no diff when the handbook is unchanged).
        # NB (T-10111): the several "NOT the land-time `_auto_rebuild_graph`" notes elsewhere (the
        # placement-manifest / AGENTS read-order GEN regions) are MANIFEST-scoped — they mark AUTHORED
        # files kept explicit-`graph build`-only so land never clobbers author edits. They do NOT apply
        # to this PURELY-DERIVED release-view, which IS regenerated here at land (do not misread them as
        # "release-view not regenerated at land").
        # T-10625: regenerate the derived graph/ audience views (SPEC-0127 §3) BEFORE the release-view
        # publishes FROM them — the ordering is load-bearing. They are purely-derived, so the SAME
        # SPEC-0074 §6 reasoning as the release-view regen above applies verbatim (the §6 doctrine is
        # artifact-class-scoped: purely-derived ⇒ regen at BOTH seams + both land sets; the T-10111 note
        # above bounds the "explicit-build-only" notes to AUTHORED files, which these are not). Without
        # this, land faithfully re-published a STALE view to `-C` consumers reading the release-view as
        # their methodology seed — the very E-0034 class T-9513 fixed for the export but not its input.
        # The helper carries its own engine-only + complete-handbook guard; idempotent (write_text_atomic
        # → no diff when the handbook is unchanged).
        _write_audience_views()
        if not _is_consumer_build() and all((ENGINE_ROOT / d).exists() for d in _release_view_docs()):
            _write_release_view()
        counts = {k: len(v) for k, v in index.items() if not k.startswith("_")}
        errors = index.get("_parse_errors") or []
        _append_event("graph_built", None, _build_graph_built_payload(
            counts, errors, trigger, duration_ms=int((time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000),
            cache=graph.LAST_INDEX_CACHE_STATE))   # T-11326: the result-cache state of this build
        # T-9715: the three report-only stale-spec/anchor drift blocks below are QUIET-BY-DEFAULT —
        # collapsed to a single summary counter-line unless YITC_GRAPH_VERBOSE is set (the full
        # per-item detail bloated console + AI context ~19-21 lines on EVERY audit/close). This is a
        # presentational default only: the blocks stay report-only stderr (SPEC-0031 report-not-block)
        # — the rebuild/conformance verdict + exit code are untouched.
        verbose = _graph_report_verbose()
        warns = index.get("_implements_warnings") or []   # T-0064: stale implements anchors
        # T-11343: PARTITION the stale-anchor rows by the owning spec's status (ONE classifier, shared
        # with the `graph build` block). LIVE = dead anchor on a still-governing spec (the headline);
        # FROZEN = an anchor on a non-active spec, accurate history (T-11340). Nothing is dropped: the
        # frozen bucket keeps its own rendered line/block on both the verbose and the quiet path.
        live_warns, frozen_warns = graph._partition_implements_warnings(warns)
        fg = index.get("_file_granular_warnings") or []   # T-9324/SPEC-0088: file-granular anchors
        fresh = index.get("_freshness_warnings") or []    # T-0213: possibly-stale (drifted) specs
        if verbose:
            if live_warns:
                print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — {len(live_warns)} stale implements "
                      f"anchor(s) on ACTIVE specs:", file=sys.stderr)
                for w in live_warns:
                    print(f"  - {w['spec']}: {w['warn']}", file=sys.stderr)
            if frozen_warns:
                print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — {len(frozen_warns)} stale implements "
                      f"anchor(s) on non-active specs — frozen history (T-11340):", file=sys.stderr)
                for w in frozen_warns:
                    print(f"  - {w['spec']} [{w.get('spec_status') or 'unknown'}]: {w['warn']}",
                          file=sys.stderr)
            if fg:
                print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — {len(fg)} file-granular anchor(s) "
                      f"(decompose to <file>#<symbol>, SPEC-0088):", file=sys.stderr)
                for w in fg:
                    print(f"  - {w['node']} ({w['kind']}): {w['warn']}", file=sys.stderr)
            if fresh:
                print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — {len(fresh)} possibly-stale spec(s) (code drifted since spec last-touch):",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                for w in fresh:
                    print(f"  - {w['spec']}: {w['warn']}", file=sys.stderr)
        elif warns or fg or fresh:
            # T-11343: the headline drift count is the LIVE one — frozen-history rows are NEVER folded
            # into it (that is what made a permanently-parked 4 train readers to ignore the counter).
            # They are reported in their OWN fragment instead, so a frozen-only corpus reads
            # "0 anchor drift(s), N frozen-history anchor(s)": live headline zero, nothing silenced.
            # T-11738 (X-1188): the SPEC-0088 file-granular rows are NOT folded into the drift count
            # either — the IDENTICAL treatment, for the identical reason. They are report-only nudges
            # to decompose <file> into <file>#<symbol>: the anchor RESOLVES, it makes no accuracy claim
            # about spec-vs-code, and `spec reverify` does not clear it. Summing them under the
            # defect-sounding name made a corpus with ZERO real drift read as N broken things (measured
            # on a consumer: 0 live, 87 file-granular, reported to an owner as "84 anchor drifts", and
            # a card filed on that false premise). The VERBOSE branch above already names the two
            # populations separately; only this collapsed line merged them. Own fragment, so a corpus
            # with no live drift reads "0 anchor drift(s), N file-granular anchor(s)" — headline zero,
            # nothing silenced (SPEC-0031 report-not-block: both stay reported, both stay report-only).
            n_anchor = len(live_warns)
            frozen_frag = (f", {len(frozen_warns)} frozen-history anchor(s)" if frozen_warns else "")
            fg_frag = (f", {len(fg)} file-granular anchor(s)" if fg else "")
            print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — {len(fresh)} possibly-stale spec(s), "
                  f"{n_anchor} anchor drift(s){frozen_frag}{fg_frag} — YITC_GRAPH_VERBOSE=1 for detail",
                  file=sys.stderr)
        # T-0230 (Part I-A): bidirectional binding-completeness report — report-not-block.
        _print_binding_report(index.get("binding_report") or {}, prefix=f"graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) — ")
        # T-0021: warn (don't block) — primary CLI action already succeeded
        if errors:
            print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) found {len(errors)} YAML parse failure(s):",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            for e in errors:
                print(f"  - {e['path']}", file=sys.stderr)
        return index   # T-0274: hand the built index back so callers (land verify) can reuse it
                       # instead of rebuilding — graph_build_index always returns a truthy dict.
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"yitc-v2: graph auto-rebuild ({trigger}) failed: {e} — index may be stale",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return None    # T-0274: None strictly signals "rebuild raised" — a land-verify FAILURE
                       # (the caller surfaces it, never masks it with a second build).


