# ── Dispatch launcher (T-0558, the W4 thin launcher) ─────────────────────────────────
# Governing home: patterns/background-session-operation.md §Dispatch (the M2 dispatch-identity
# contract — SPEC-0005 rule 8 "one home"; NOT duplicated into a spec). This is the THIN launcher
# realizing M2(b): ONE blessed launch = fresh session id → scrub ALL identity carriers (the T-0554
# SESSION_IDENTITY_REGISTRY, single-SoT — NOT a hardcoded var list) → assign the `expected`
# session-identity as an EXPLICIT contract input → spawn the background worker → emit ONE dispatch
# journal event carrying `expected`. It is a LAUNCHER, NOT an orchestrator: NO selection / ordering
# / retry / wait / ownership FSM (CHARTER non-goal #7 — that would slide into pipeline orchestration;
# selection stays the controller's manual §Launch duty). The provider binding (the headless `claude
# -p` invocation, `--session-id`, the env scrub of the registry carriers) lives in CODE only
# (CHARTER §P4b); the rule surface stays provider-neutral. Trial mechanism, owner-invoked-only —
# CHARTER §6 is not yet amended (orchestrate posture in trial).
#
# LIST extension (T-0580): `--task` is REPEATABLE — the launcher loops the owner-given list,
# launching each worker SEQUENTIALLY in one invocation with its own fresh session id + the full M2
# scrub-then-set + its own `bg_dispatch_launched` append. The loop is the owner's EXPLICIT list, NOT
# selection/ordering/retry (still non-goal #7). Because the loop is synchronous (no concurrency),
# each append completes before the next iteration — the appends are SERIALIZED, so launching several
# in one go avoids the concurrent-append loss that separate concurrent invocations risked
# (`startup-event-emit-silent-loss-concurrent-main-checkout`).
#
# Event `bg_dispatch_launched` (the launch marker of the bg_dispatch_* family — peer of
# `bg_dispatch_halted`; lives in code + the §Dispatch pattern, NOT the SPEC-0025 catalog, which is
# an open enum): bound to the MAIN checkout's journal (append-only, no worktree — D-0049). Schema:
#   task_id: T-XXXX
#   data: {dispatch: <tag=task>, expected: <assigned worker session_ref>, provider: <name>,
#          pid: <spawned pid>, log: <worker log path>}
# The envelope `session_ref` is the CONTROLLER's (resolved by _append_event); `data.expected` is the
# worker's launcher-assigned ref — so `data.expected != envelope session_ref` is the AC1 distinctness
# probe. The `expected` value is the launcher-assigned EXPLICIT contract input the worker fail-closed
# self-check (T-0561) compares against — carried to the worker as YITC_EXPECTED_SESSION_REF, a var
# DISTINCT from the session_ref-resolution carriers (never env-resolved as identity; external-review (3)).


# === dispatch verb-family (T-9335): bodies extracted to bin/lib/dispatch.py. Host keeps the PROVIDER
#     BINDING (_spawn_claude_worker + the DISPATCH_PROVIDERS registry — the AUDIT_PROVIDERS precedent:
#     the registry contract carries no injected-dep slot, and tests MUTATE yitc.DISPATCH_PROVIDERS) +
#     thin residue under the historical names (injecting wrappers / re-export aliases) so every
#     yitc.<name> caller + monkeypatch stays honored at call time. Engine: bin/lib/dispatch.py.
