def _absorb_trailing_reason_words(parser: argparse.ArgumentParser) -> None:
    """T-11058 — let the BARE doctrine form parse when a flag arrives BEFORE the reason words.

    The defect (PRE-EXISTING since T-10792 — proven by `git log -S` on the `--stage` registration,
    the verb's own shipping commit, so NOT a T-11008 regression): argparse ends a `nargs="*"`
    positional run at the first optional it consumes mid-argv, and every word after that becomes an
    "unrecognized argument" the TOP-LEVEL parser rejects. So `blocked-on-land T-X --stage land <words>`
    and `blocked-on-land T-X --from-stdin <words>` both died at the parser, while the same words
    placed BEFORE the flags parsed fine. That is exactly the form SPEC-0103 §3 and the dispatch
    preamble tell a BLOCKED worker to type, so the failure lands at the worst possible moment: a
    parser error where an escalation should have been recorded, which the controller reads as a
    silent death rather than a halt — the very conflation this verb exists to end.

    WHY NOT `nargs=argparse.REMAINDER` (the card's other candidate): MEASURED, it is worse than the
    bug. REMAINDER swallows every following flag INTO the reason — `--reason flag positional` leaves
    `reason_flag=None`, `--from-stdin words` leaves `from_stdin=False` — silently inverting the
    precedence rule and disarming the stdin/argv conflict refusal. It buys AC1 by breaking AC2.

    WHAT THIS DOES instead: after the subparser's own normal parse (all flags keep TODAY's semantics
    — `--reason` still wins, `--from-stdin` still sets, `--stage` still consumes its value), the
    leftover strings are appended to `reason_words` in ARGV ORDER, dash-leading tokens included
    verbatim, and the leftovers list is emptied so the top level has nothing to reject. Hooked onto
    THIS subparser instance because the subparsers action calls the CHILD's `parse_known_args` and
    hands its leftovers up — so `build_parser().parse_args(...)` and the real CLI fix together, with
    no change to `main()`'s parse and no effect on any other verb's error handling.

    ACCEPTED CONSEQUENCE, this verb only: a MISTYPED flag after the task id becomes reason text
    rather than a parser error. The right trade — the reason here is bare unquoted prose whose words
    are unknowable in advance, and a mistyped flag lands loudly in the recorded reason the controller
    reads, whereas the status quo loses the whole escalation."""
    inner = parser.parse_known_args

    def parse_known_args(args=None, namespace=None):
        ns, extras = inner(args, namespace)
        if extras:
            ns.reason_words = list(getattr(ns, "reason_words", None) or []) + list(extras)
            extras = []
        return ns, extras

    parser.parse_known_args = parse_known_args


