_STDOUT_DISCARDED_WARNED = False   # T-11963: the once-per-process latch for `_warn_stdout_discarded`


def _warn_stdout_discarded(verb_label: str, node_id: str) -> None:
    """The at-the-fetch signal for a CREDIT-BEARING fetch whose stdout went to the null device (T-11963).

    THE SAME JUDGEMENT ALREADY EXISTS — at the wrong end. `gates.py` (T-11576 / <project> X-1101) tells a
    reader that a doc's only receipt was recorded `stdout_delivered: False` — but it says so AT A LATER
    VERB'S REFUSAL, after the fetch, the wait and the failed verb are already spent. The measurement is
    that a diagnostic there does not prevent the shape: with it shipped, class C5a rose 280 -> 324 in one
    day, and 324 of its 325 all-time rows fall inside a single week (T-11877 reading (4)). So the same
    fact is stated HERE, at the moment it becomes true, where the cure is still cheap.

    SCOPED TO CREDIT-BEARING RECEIPTS, which is why the caller gates on `node_id`. `_fetched_spec_ids`
    credits a `cli_invoked` row by its `node_id`, and `_emit_cli_invoked` sets that key on exactly three
    legs — a `graph query` point lookup, `--help` (the verb inventory), `session start` (the seed
    receipt). A row without one credits nothing, so a notice there would announce a loss that cannot
    occur, and a signal that cries wolf gets skimmed (the `_stdout_was_delivered` discriminator note
    makes the same argument about `isatty()`).

    ONCE PER PROCESS — the latch is load-bearing, not tidiness. The unit of "one discarded stdout" is the
    PROCESS: one process has one fd 1. The stage-bundle delivery loop calls `_emit_cli_invoked` once per
    delivered spec inside a SINGLE process, so without the latch one discarded stdout would produce N
    identical notices. The latch makes the AC2 bound ("exactly one notice per discarded stdout") hold by
    construction rather than by the accident of how many receipts a verb happens to emit.

    THE GATE-SIDE DIAGNOSTIC STAYS — recorded disposition, so the two never double-report by design:
      * They cannot both be a notice about ONE discard. This fires in the process that DISCARDED, at
        fetch time; the gate note fires in a LATER, DIFFERENT process and is a per-REFUSAL explanation
        ("this refusal is a delivery miss, not a missing fetch"), not a second report of the discard.
      * The gate note is NOT redundant even where they describe the same underlying receipt: the
        canonical invocation is `--help >/dev/null 2>&1`, which discards STDERR TOO — so THIS notice is
        written and thrown away in exactly the case that produced X-1101. Retiring the gate note would
        reopen the refusal loop it was built to close. T-11877's "the channel the redirect did not
        capture" is true of `>/dev/null` and false of `>/dev/null 2>&1`; this notice is the cheap early
        cure for the first, and the gate note remains the only cure for the second.

    STDERR only, exit status untouched (the fetch itself succeeded and its bytes were written) — same
    contract as `_warn_help_receipt_uncredited` below, so no output-parsing consumer is affected. The
    remediation line is reconstructed from the LIVE invocation, never a hardcoded `bin/yitc-v2` prefix,
    so an absolute-engine or `-C <consumer>` call reads back a command the operator can actually paste."""
    global _STDOUT_DISCARDED_WARNED
    if _STDOUT_DISCARDED_WARNED:
        return
    _STDOUT_DISCARDED_WARNED = True
    argv = list(sys.argv)
    print("\n".join([
        f"yitc-v2: WARNING — this `{verb_label}` fetch grants NO read-gate pass: its stdout was "
        f"DISCARDED (fd 1 is the null device), so the receipt for {node_id} is recorded "
        "stdout_delivered=false and `_fetched_spec_ids` will skip it (T-11411).",
        "  consequence: a later gated verb will REFUSE for a fetch you did run — the gate checks "
        "DELIVERY, not comprehension, and bytes sent to /dev/null are not delivery either.",
        "  fix — re-run this exact command with stdout ATTACHED (do not redirect or discard it):",
        f"    {shlex.join(argv)}",
    ]), file=sys.stderr)


